# Payverge self-host settings, read by docker-compose.yml in this directory.
#
# The easy way: ./install.sh writes .env for you, generates every secret and
# starts the stack. By hand:
#
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#   # fill in the "Required" block, then:
#   docker compose up -d
#
# Only the "Required" block must be filled in. Everything under "Optional" is
# commented out and already has a working default; uncomment a line to change
# it. Format: KEY=value, no spaces around "=". Compose expands "$" inside
# unquoted values, so wrap a value that contains "$" in single quotes.
#
# After editing: docker compose up -d   (recreates only what changed)
# Full reference: README.md here, and docs/self-hosting/ in the repository.

# ==== Required ===============================================================

# Public host name, without scheme or path (payments.example.com). Its DNS
# A/AAAA records must point at this server, and ports 80 and 443 must be
# reachable from the internet so Caddy can get a certificate.
# DOMAIN=localhost runs a local trial with a certificate from Caddy's own CA.
DOMAIN=

# The first platform admin, created on the first boot. The password needs at
# least 12 characters and 5 different characters. Remove ADMIN_PASSWORD once
# the stack has started (install.sh does this for you): from then on the
# password lives only in the database. Lost it? See README.md "Admin access".
ADMIN_EMAIL=
ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Secrets. Generate each one once and keep it; back up this file.
#   DB_PASSWORD        openssl rand -hex 24
#   JWT_SECRET_KEY     openssl rand -base64 48
#   PLUGIN_SECRET_KEY  openssl rand -hex 32
# DB_PASSWORD is applied when the database is first created; changing it later
# locks the backend out. Changing JWT_SECRET_KEY signs everyone out. Changing
# PLUGIN_SECRET_KEY makes the stored payment-provider credentials unreadable.
DB_PASSWORD=
JWT_SECRET_KEY=
PLUGIN_SECRET_KEY=

# Image version: the release this file shipped with. Change it to upgrade
# when you choose; "latest" follows the newest stable release on every pull.
# See README.md "Upgrades".
# x-release-please-start-version
PAYVERGE_VERSION=1.0.1
# x-release-please-end

# Optional services, comma-separated: backup, backup-offsite, minio.
# "backup" takes a nightly database + uploads backup into ./backups.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=backup

# ==== Optional ===============================================================

# ---- TLS, ports and the edge ------------------------------------------------
# Let's Encrypt account email: expiry warnings and account recovery.
# ACME_EMAIL=you@example.com
#
# Host ports Caddy publishes. Changing HTTPS_PORT also needs
# PUBLIC_URL=https://<DOMAIN>:<port>, and public certificates need 80 and 443.
# HTTP_PORT=80
# HTTPS_PORT=443
#
# The canonical origin. Defaults to https://<DOMAIN>.
# PUBLIC_URL=
#
# Behind Cloudflare's proxy (orange cloud, SSL mode "Full (strict)"). Caddy
# then takes the client IP from CF-Connecting-IP, but only from Cloudflare's
# published ranges. See README.md "Cloudflare".
# EDGE=cloudflare
#
# Another reverse proxy or load balancer in front of Caddy: its address ranges
# so Caddy takes the client IP from X-Forwarded-For. Format: IPv4/IPv6
# addresses or CIDRs separated by SPACES (not commas), quoted when there is
# more than one; nothing broader than /8 (IPv4) or /16 (IPv6). install.sh rejects anything else.
# Caddy reads that header right to left and stops at the first address outside
# these ranges, so list every proxy hop and nothing broader.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS="10.0.0.0/24 fd00::/64"
#
# Another web server on this host already owns ports 80 and 443: it
# terminates TLS and forwards plain HTTP to Caddy, which then serves
# http://<DOMAIN> with no certificate and no redirect. Needs EDGE=none,
# loopback ports and TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS; see README.md "Behind another web server".
# TLS_TERMINATION=proxy
# HTTP_PORT=127.0.0.1:8080
# HTTPS_PORT=127.0.0.1:8443
#
# Strict-Transport-Security that Caddy sends for DOMAIN (it replaces any value
# the apps send). The default covers DOMAIN only. Add "; includeSubDomains"
# only when every subdomain of DOMAIN serves HTTPS; max-age=0 withdraws it.
# HSTS_POLICY=max-age=31536000
#
# Send Strict-Transport-Security from the backend itself. Behind this Caddy it
# changes nothing (HSTS_POLICY wins); it matters only when the backend is
# reached without it.
# FORCE_HSTS=false
#
# The private network Caddy, the backend and the frontend share. The backend
# and frontend trust X-Forwarded-For only from it (TRUSTED_PROXIES and
# FRONTEND_TRUSTED_PROXIES default to it). Change it only when this range is
# already in use on the host, then: docker compose down && docker compose up -d
# EDGE_SUBNET=172.30.0.0/24

# ---- Sign-ups and demo data -------------------------------------------------
# invite (default): new operators need an invite code from a platform admin.
# open: anyone can sign up. closed: no new accounts.
# REGISTRATION_MODE=invite
#
# Seed demo restaurants owned by ADMIN_EMAIL on boot (idempotent). Remove them
# later with: docker compose exec backend /app/server demo reset --all
# DEMO_DATA=false
#
# The venue served at "/" (numeric id, /b/<slug> custom URL or business_id
# slug). Empty =
# the only published venue, or a directory when several are published; with
# none published "/" goes to the operator sign-in (/dashboard). install.sh
# --demo sets it to bodegon-mesa-larga, the main demo restaurant.
# PRIMARY_VENUE=

# ---- Branding (empty = the Payverge defaults) -------------------------------
# PRODUCT_NAME=
# COMPANY_NAME=
# COMPANY_ADDRESS=
# LEGAL_ENTITY=
# Your own terms / privacy pages (absolute https URL). When set, /terms-and-
# conditions and /privacy-policy redirect there instead of the generic template.
# LEGAL_TERMS_URL=
# LEGAL_PRIVACY_URL=
# LOGO_URL=
# BRAND_COLOR=
#
# Contacts shown to guests and operators, and in /.well-known/security.txt.
# SUPPORT_EMAIL=
# SECURITY_EMAIL=

# ---- Email ------------------------------------------------------------------
# With nothing set, mail is not delivered. The backend logs metadata only
# (template, recipient count and hash).
#   docker compose logs backend | grep -i email
# EMAIL_LOG_CONTENT=true also logs recipients, subject, body preview and links.
# In production that needs EMAIL_PROVIDER_LOG_ALLOW_PRODUCTION=true or startup fails.
# EMAIL_LOG_CONTENT=true
# EMAIL_PROVIDER_LOG_ALLOW_PRODUCTION=true
#
# Your own mailbox or relay:
# EMAIL_PROVIDER=smtp
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USERNAME=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# SMTP_FROM=noreply@example.com
# SMTP_TLS=auto
#
# Resend (verified sending domain required):
# EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# FROM_EMAIL=noreply@example.com
# FROM_EMAIL_UPDATES=updates@example.com
# EMAIL_ALLOWED_FROM_DOMAINS=example.com
# EMAIL_SPF_DOMAIN=example.com
# EMAIL_DKIM_DOMAIN=example.com
# EMAIL_RETURN_PATH_DOMAIN=example.com
# EMAIL_DMARC_POLICY=quarantine
#
# Postmark:
# EMAIL_PROVIDER=postmark
# EMAIL_API_KEY=
# FROM_EMAIL=noreply@example.com
# FROM_EMAIL_UPDATES=updates@example.com
#
# auto (default): verification required whenever mail is actually delivered.
# EMAIL_VERIFICATION=auto
# EMAIL_REPLY_TO=
#
# Per-business send budgets; empty = the defaults in docs/self-hosting/email.md
# (a negative value means unlimited, 0 blocks that scope).
# EMAIL_TENANT_DAILY_CAP=
# EMAIL_TENANT_DEMO_DAILY_CAP=
# EMAIL_TENANT_RECIPIENT_DAILY_CAP=
# EMAIL_TENANT_RECIPIENT_GLOBAL_DAILY_CAP=
# EMAIL_TENANT_RECIPIENT_GLOBAL_ALL_DAILY_CAP=
# EMAIL_TENANT_RESERVATION_DAILY_CAP=
# EMAIL_TENANT_RESERVATION_RECIPIENT_DAILY_CAP=
# EMAIL_TENANT_DEDUPE_MINUTES=
#
# Signs unsubscribe links. Empty = derived from JWT_SECRET_KEY; set it so a
# JWT_SECRET_KEY rotation does not invalidate links already sent.
# UNSUBSCRIBE_TOKEN_SECRET=

# ---- AI (menu import, AI waiter, director console) -------------------------
# Off until a model endpoint is configured. Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint
# works; models are named per task below. See docs/self-hosting/ai.md.
#
# OpenRouter:
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
#
# Ollama on this host (the backend can reach the host as host.docker.internal;
# make Ollama listen on more than 127.0.0.1, e.g. OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0):
# LLM_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434/v1
#
# Any other OpenAI-compatible endpoint (vLLM, LiteLLM, OpenAI, ...):
# LLM_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
# LLM_API_KEY=
#
# Model ids. The defaults are OpenRouter ids: set all of them when
# LLM_BASE_URL points elsewhere.
# OPENROUTER_MODEL_CHAT=google/gemini-2.5-flash
# OPENROUTER_MODEL_MENU=google/gemini-2.5-flash
# OPENROUTER_MODEL_IMAGE=google/gemini-2.5-flash-image
# OPENROUTER_MODEL_DIRECTOR=google/gemini-2.5-flash
# OPENROUTER_MODEL_GUARDRAIL=google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite
#
# Daily spend ceilings in USD (UTC day): per restaurant and whole instance.
# Empty = derived: global = max($20, 2 x per restaurant).
# AI_DAILY_BUDGET_USD=5
# AI_BUDGET_GLOBAL_USD_DAY=
# Sub-cap inside the global ceiling for guest waiter turns at every
# restaurant (empty = derived from AI_DAILY_BUDGET_USD).
# AI_BUDGET_GUEST_POOL_USD_DAY=
#
# Prices for a model missing from the built-in table, as
# model=input/output USD per million tokens, comma-separated. An unpriced
# configured model stops AI at startup.
# OPENROUTER_PRICES=
#
# Guardrail posture when the guardrail model is down: empty = fail closed
# in production; true/false overrides.
# GUARDRAIL_STRICT=
#
# Menu translation and place lookup (Google Cloud keys).
# GOOGLE_TRANSLATE_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY=

# ---- File storage -----------------------------------------------------------
# Default: uploads stay on the "storage" Docker volume (STORAGE_DRIVER=local),
# served by the backend at /media/*, and are part of every backup.
#
# S3 or any S3-compatible service. Both buckets are required; the protected
# one holds private files (receipts, exports) and must not be public.
# STORAGE_DRIVER=s3
# S3_BUCKET=payverge-public
# S3_PROTECTED_BUCKET=payverge-protected
# AWS_REGION=us-east-1
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY=
# AWS_SECRET_KEY=
# AWS_PROTECTED_ACCESS_KEY=
# AWS_PROTECTED_SECRET_KEY=
# Empty for AWS; https://... for R2, B2, Wasabi:
# S3_ENDPOINT=
# S3_PROTECTED_ENDPOINT=
# Public URL of the public bucket or its CDN:
# S3_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=
# S3_PROTECTED_BASE_URL=
# S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=false
# The S3_PUBLIC_BASE_URL origin, for the CSP:
# MEDIA_ORIGINS=
#
# Bundled MinIO instead (no cloud account). Add "minio" to COMPOSE_PROFILES;
# minio-init creates both buckets and the access key below on first start.
# MinIO is AGPL-3.0 and runs unmodified as a separate service.
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=backup,minio
# STORAGE_DRIVER=s3
# MINIO_ROOT_USER=payverge-root
# install.sh fills this in; by hand: openssl rand -hex 24. MinIO refuses to
# start without it (an empty one would mean MinIO's well-known default).
# MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=
# S3_ENDPOINT=http://minio:9000
# S3_PROTECTED_ENDPOINT=http://minio:9000
# S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true
# S3_BUCKET=payverge-public
# S3_PROTECTED_BUCKET=payverge-protected
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY=payverge-app
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 24
# AWS_SECRET_KEY=
# AWS_PROTECTED_ACCESS_KEY=payverge-app
# Same value as AWS_SECRET_KEY:
# AWS_PROTECTED_SECRET_KEY=
# Another MinIO image (default: Chainguard's, pinned by digest in docker-compose.yml):
# MINIO_IMAGE=cgr.dev/chainguard/minio:latest

# ---- Guest payments ---------------------------------------------------------
# Restaurants connect their own Stripe / PayPal / MercadoPago accounts in the
# dashboard. These platform-level values are only needed for the OAuth
# "connect" buttons and the platform webhooks.
#
# In production every card provider stays OFF until its switch is true. Turn
# one on only after testing it in that provider's sandbox with your own test
# account.
# PAYMENT_PROVIDER_STRIPE_ENABLED=
# PAYMENT_PROVIDER_PAYPAL_ENABLED=
# PAYMENT_PROVIDER_MERCADOPAGO_ENABLED=
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# STRIPE_PLUGIN_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# STRIPE_CONNECT_CLIENT_ID=
# STRIPE_CONNECT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# PAYPAL_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# MERCADOPAGO_CLIENT_ID=
# MERCADOPAGO_CLIENT_SECRET=
# MERCADOPAGO_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# MAX_PAYMENT_AMOUNT_CENTS=

# ---- USDC on Base -----------------------------------------------------------
# The backend verifies USDC transfers on Base mainnet through a public RPC by
# default. A private RPC (with an API key) stays on the backend; the browser
# uses PUBLIC_RPC_URL.
# RPC_URL=https://mainnet.base.org
# PUBLIC_RPC_URL=
# NETWORK=base
# USDC_MIN_CONFIRMATIONS=
# Signs locked crypto price quotes. Empty = derived from JWT_SECRET_KEY.
# CRYPTO_QUOTE_SECRET=

# ---- Google sign-in ---------------------------------------------------------
# OAuth client of type "Web application"; authorized redirect URI:
# https://<DOMAIN>/api/v1/auth/google/callback
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---- Telegram (staff notifications) -----------------------------------------
# TELEGRAM_TOKEN=
# TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME=
# Required with TELEGRAM_TOKEN. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# TELEGRAM_NOTIFICATION_EVENTS=
# TELEGRAM_ESCALATION_BOT_TOKEN=
# TELEGRAM_ESCALATION_CHAT_ID=

# ---- WhatsApp ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Not in the published images: the WhatsApp channel links GPL-3.0 code, so it
# is compiled in only when you build the backend yourself with GO_TAGS=whatsapp
# (docker-compose.build.yml). Read docs/self-hosting/whatsapp.md first.
# GO_TAGS=whatsapp
# WHATSAPP_ENABLED=false

# ---- Web push ---------------------------------------------------------------
# npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com

# ---- Error tracking, analytics and metrics ----------------------------------
# All off by default; nothing is sent anywhere.
# SENTRY_DSN=
# FRONTEND_SENTRY_DSN=
# POSTHOG_API_KEY=
# POSTHOG_HOST=
# POSTHOG_KEY=
# Prometheus metrics: GET http://backend:8080/metrics with this bearer token.
# Not routed through Caddy; scrape it from a container on the edge network.
# METRICS_TOKEN=
# Comma-separated tokens, all accepted, for rotation: add the new one, move
# the scrapers over, then drop the old one.
# METRICS_TOKENS=
# Bearer token for the detailed /api/v1/health/ready body (components,
# uptime). Unset, the endpoint answers only ready or not ready.
# HEALTH_DETAIL_TOKEN=
#
# Let search engines index the public pages (off on a fresh instance).
# SEO_INDEXING=false

# ---- Admin MCP server (AI agents operating this instance) -------------------
# Store only the SHA-256 of the token here. See tools/payverge-admin-mcp/ in
# the repository.
# PAYVERGE_ADMIN_MCP_TOKEN_SHA256=
# PAYVERGE_ADMIN_MCP_ALLOWED_IPS=

# ---- Backups (profile "backup") ---------------------------------------------
# HH:MM, 24-hour, in TZ:
# BACKUP_TIME=03:30
# TZ=UTC
# BACKUP_KEEP_DAILY=7
# BACKUP_KEEP_WEEKLY=4
# BACKUP_KEEP_MONTHLY=6
# BACKUP_DIR=./backups
# Numeric host user and group that own the sets, so they can be listed and
# copied without root. install.sh sets them to the user who ran it (not when
# that is root). Empty: the sets stay owned by root.
# BACKUP_UID=1000
# BACKUP_GID=1000
# Tries per volume archive (uploads, MinIO) when a file changes during the copy.
# BACKUP_ARCHIVE_ATTEMPTS=3
# The backup service turns unhealthy (docker compose ps) when the last run
# failed or none succeeded for this many hours.
# BACKUP_MAX_AGE_HOURS=26
#
# Off-site copy (profile "backup-offsite"): any rclone remote, configured in
# ./rclone/rclone.conf. See README.md "Off-site copies".
# RCLONE_REMOTE=offsite:payverge-backups
# copy keeps sets pruned here; sync mirrors the pruning:
# RCLONE_MODE=copy
# RCLONE_INTERVAL_SECONDS=3600

# ---- Database ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The application's role: it owns DB_NAME and is not a superuser (the
# superuser "postgres" has no password and connects only from inside the
# postgres container). Read on the first start only; it cannot be "postgres".
# DB_USER=payverge
# DB_NAME=payverge
